('127.402.d.11.B',4,'Using the same procedure for every trial makes results:','["shorter", "secret", "comparable", "random"]'::jsonb,2,'Consistent tests allow comparison.','original'),
('127.402.d.11.C',3,'Evaluating a prototype using data means looking at:','["guesses", "rumors", "feelings only", "measurements rather than only opinions"]'::jsonb,3,'Data supports evaluation.','original'),
('127.402.d.11.C',4,'A critique of a prototype should be:','["specific and based on observations", "vague", "hostile", "personal"]'::jsonb,0,'Useful feedback is specific.','original'),
('127.402.d.11.D',3,'A focus group is a small group of people who:','["pay for the product", "discuss a product and give feedback", "sell the product", "build the product"]'::jsonb,1,'Focus groups gather user opinion.','original'),
('127.402.d.11.D',4,'A user test where people try a prototype while the team watches is a:','["tax audit", "patent search", "usability test", "production run"]'::jsonb,2,'Usability tests show real use.','original'),
('127.402.d.11.E',3,'A success of a prototype is a feature that:','["is hidden", "is ignored", "breaks", "meets a stated criterion"]'::jsonb,3,'Successes match goals.','original'),
('127.402.d.11.E',4,'If a prototype holds 8 kg but the goal was 10 kg, this result is best described as:','["a failure to meet one criterion", "a perfect design", "a total success", "irrelevant"]'::jsonb,0,'Compare results with criteria.','original'),
('127.402.d.12.A',3,'A tool such as a failure mode analysis helps a team to:','["sell products", "find ways a design could fail", "pick colors", "write slogans"]'::jsonb,1,'Analysis predicts failure.','original'),
('127.402.d.12.A',4,'A fishbone (cause and effect) diagram helps a team to analyze:','["the budget only", "the schedule only", "the causes of a problem", "the logo"]'::jsonb,2,'Fishbone diagrams map causes.','original'),
('127.402.d.12.B',3,'After analyzing flaws, the team returns to earlier steps to:','["delete notes", "quit", "hide it", "improve and optimize the design"]'::jsonb,3,'Iterate for improvement.','original'),
('127.402.d.12.B',4,'Repeating a test after a design change allows the team to check whether the change:','["improved performance", "was popular", "was free", "was legal"]'::jsonb,0,'Retesting confirms impact.','original'),
('127.402.d.12.C',3,'Evaluating the impact of a solution means asking how well it:','["looks", "solves the original problem", "sells", "costs less"]'::jsonb,1,'Check against the problem.','original'),
('127.402.d.12.C',4,'A solution may also have impacts on people and the:','["weather report", "calendar", "environment", "sports score"]'::jsonb,2,'Consider side effects.','original'),
('127.402.d.13.A',3,'When presenting the experience of working on a design project, a student should share what the team:','["wore", "bought", "ate", "did and learned"]'::jsonb,3,'Reflect on the work.','original'),
('127.402.d.13.A',4,'A clear presentation of a project is organized with a beginning, middle, and:','["end with conclusions", "joke", "blank page", "random order"]'::jsonb,0,'Structure helps audiences.','original'),
('127.402.d.13.B',3,'Feedback on a team''s use of the design process helps them to:','["hide results", "improve next time", "lose track", "stop learning"]'::jsonb,1,'Feedback supports growth.','original'),
('127.402.d.13.B',4,'Evaluating feedback means deciding which comments are:','["loudest", "rudest", "useful and based on evidence", "first"]'::jsonb,2,'Judge feedback quality.','original'),
('127.402.d.13.C',3,'A student who lists skills such as teamwork and communication gained from a project is doing a:','["repair", "review of prices", "purchase", "reflection"]'::jsonb,3,'Reflection captures learning.','original'),
('127.402.d.13.C',4,'Identifying areas for growth after a project means naming skills to:','["practice and improve", "avoid", "hide", "forget"]'::jsonb,0,'Growth areas set goals.','original'),
('127.403.d.1',3,'An engineer is asked to approve a part that fails a safety test. The ethical response is to:','["hide the result", "refuse approval and report the failure", "change the test", "approve it quietly"]'::jsonb,1,'Ethics require honesty about safety.','original'),
('127.403.d.1',4,'Engineering ethics research often cites the Challenger shuttle disaster, in which warnings about a faulty seal were:','["fully heeded", "solved in time", "overruled by schedule pressure", "never given"]'::jsonb,2,'Engineers warned but launch went ahead.','original'),
('127.403.d.2.A',3,'Applying an engineering design process to a real problem begins with:','["building at once", "ignoring users", "selling", "defining the problem"]'::jsonb,3,'Define first.','original'),
('127.403.d.2.A',4,'Stage-gate reviews in a design process make sure that a project:','["meets criteria before moving on", "skips testing", "never changes", "ends early"]'::jsonb,0,'Gates check readiness.','original'),
('127.403.d.2.B',3,'A project''s scope describes:','["the team uniforms", "what is and is not included in the work", "only the cost", "the color"]'::jsonb,1,'Scope sets boundaries.','original'),
('127.403.d.2.B',4,'Scope creep happens when a project:','["has no budget", "finishes early", "keeps adding work beyond the original plan", "shrinks"]'::jsonb,2,'Uncontrolled additions cause delays.','original'),
('127.403.d.2.C',3,'A stakeholder is a person or group who:','["builds only", "designs only", "pays only", "is affected by or has an interest in the project"]'::jsonb,3,'Stakeholders include users and funders.','original'),
('127.403.d.2.C',4,'Consulting neighbors about a new factory''s noise is an example of considering:','["stakeholder concerns", "profit only", "marketing", "taxes only"]'::jsonb,0,'Stakeholders influence decisions.','original'),
('127.403.d.2.D',3,'In engineering, failure can be useful because it:','["is always a waste", "teaches what must be improved", "ends learning", "proves nothing"]'::jsonb,1,'Learning from failure drives progress.','original'),
('127.403.d.2.D',4,'An engineer who studies why a test sample cracked is practicing:','["advertising", "sales", "failure analysis", "scheduling"]'::jsonb,2,'Failure analysis finds root causes.','original'),
('127.403.d.3.A',3,'The phases of a project include initiating, planning, executing, monitoring, and:','["hiding", "advertising", "printing", "closing"]'::jsonb,3,'Closing completes a project.','original'),
('127.403.d.3.A',4,'Monitoring a project during execution means:','["comparing progress with the plan", "hiding delays", "ignoring the plan", "stopping work"]'::jsonb,0,'Monitoring finds variances.','original'),
('127.403.d.3.B',3,'The project manager is responsible for:','["buying lunch", "coordinating the team and keeping the project on track", "painting", "building every part"]'::jsonb,1,'Managers coordinate.','original'),
('127.403.d.3.B',4,'A team member''s role in a project is usually described in:','["a random task list", "a guess", "a defined set of responsibilities", "a joke"]'::jsonb,2,'Clear roles prevent overlap.','original'),
('127.403.d.3.C',3,'A Gantt chart is a tool that shows:','["safety rules", "only budgets", "blueprints", "tasks along a timeline"]'::jsonb,3,'Gantt charts schedule tasks.','original'),
('127.403.d.3.C',4,'A project management tool that lets team members track tasks and share files is:','["project management software", "a ruler", "a calculator only", "a drill press"]'::jsonb,0,'Software coordinates work.','original'),
('127.403.d.3.D',3,'A system for tracking documents helps a team to:','["lose files", "find the latest version of a file", "delete work", "hide edits"]'::jsonb,1,'Version control prevents confusion.','original'),
('127.403.d.3.D',4,'Naming files with version numbers and dates prevents:','["backups", "saving", "working from outdated drawings", "printing"]'::jsonb,2,'Clear versions avoid errors.','original'),
('127.403.d.3.E',3,'A deliverable is a:','["tool", "meeting", "cost", "product or document the team must provide"]'::jsonb,3,'Deliverables are outputs.','original'),
('127.403.d.3.E',4,'If a requirement changes late in a project, the team should assess the effect on the:','["schedule and budget", "weather", "lunch menu", "uniform"]'::jsonb,0,'Changes ripple.','original'),
('127.403.d.3.F',3,'A project budget includes the cost of materials, equipment, and:','["luck", "labor", "weather", "hobbies"]'::jsonb,1,'Budgets sum all costs.','original'),
('127.403.d.3.F',4,'If materials cost $400, equipment $250, and labor $600, the total budget is:','["$1,350", "$850", "$1,250", "$1,150"]'::jsonb,2,'400 + 250 + 600 = 1250.','original'),
('127.403.d.3.G',3,'Management of change (MOC) is a process used to:','["skip safety", "block progress", "avoid documentation", "review and approve changes before they are made"]'::jsonb,3,'MOC controls risk.','original'),
('127.403.d.3.G',4,'A change to a chemical plant''s valve type should go through MOC because it may affect:','["safety and operations", "uniforms", "lunch", "parking"]'::jsonb,0,'Changes can create hazards.','original'),
('127.403.d.4.A',3,'Decomposing a problem means breaking it into:','["nothing", "smaller, manageable parts", "bigger parts", "random pieces"]'::jsonb,1,'Decomposition simplifies.','original'),
('127.403.d.4.A',4,'To plan an automatic watering system, a team might split the problem into sensing, deciding, and:','["naming", "painting", "delivering water", "advertising"]'::jsonb,2,'Each part is a subproblem.','original'),
('127.403.d.4.B',3,'A pattern in a sequence of numbers such as 2, 4, 6, 8 allows a programmer to:','["avoid math", "stop the program", "delete data", "predict the next number"]'::jsonb,3,'Patterns support prediction.','original'),
('127.403.d.4.B',4,'A repeating pattern in a manufacturing process can be automated using:','["a loop in a program", "a variable name only", "a typo", "a comment"]'::jsonb,0,'Repetition suggests loops.','original'),
('127.403.d.4.C',3,'Abstraction means focusing on the:','["all details", "important details and ignoring the rest", "none", "smallest detail"]'::jsonb,1,'Abstraction simplifies.','original'),
('127.403.d.4.C',4,'A function that is called by name without needing to know its internal steps is an example of:','["bug", "error", "abstraction", "loop"]'::jsonb,2,'Functions hide detail.','original')
